  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 192,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAnnex I. External Sector Assessment\nOverall Assessment: The external position in 2021 was broadly in line with the level implied by medium-term fundamentals and desirable policies. The\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 192,
      "end_char": 339,
      "text": "rebalancing of Mexico’s external position in 2021 was led by the economic reopening and recovery, domestically and elsewhere, and a smaller fiscal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 339,
      "end_char": 483,
      "text": "policy gap. The latter reflected the narrowing of the wide cross-country differences in the magnitude of pandemic-related fiscal policy support\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 483,
      "end_char": 613,
      "text": "compared with the previous year. The current account deficit is expected to stabilize around 1 percent of GDP in the medium term.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 613,
      "end_char": 757,
      "text": "Potential Policy Responses: While Mexico’s external position at present is broadly in balance, further structural reforms to address investment\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 757,
      "end_char": 905,
      "text": "obstacles are critical to boost growth and exports in the medium and long terms and to maintain external sustainability. The reforms should include\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 905,
      "end_char": 1052,
      "text": "tackling economic informality and governance gaps, private sector participation in energy, and reforming Pemex’s business strategy and governance.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1052,
      "end_char": 1202,
      "text": "The floating exchange rate should continue to serve as a shock absorber, with FX interventions used only to prevent disorderly market conditions. The\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1202,
      "end_char": 1427,
      "text": "IMF’s Flexible Credit Line continues to provide an added buffer against global tail risks.\nForeign Asset Background. Mexico’s NIIP is projected to improve from –41 percent of GDP in 2021 to around –35 percent of GDP over the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1427,
      "end_char": 1575,
      "text": "and Liability medium term, driven mainly by the decline in foreign liabilities. Foreign assets in 2021 were mostly direct investment (18 percent of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1575,
      "end_char": 1715,
      "text": "Position and GDP) and international reserves (16 percent of GDP). Foreign liabilities were mostly direct investment (49 percent of GDP) and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 1715,
      "end_char": 1941,
      "text": "Trajectory portfolio investment (39 percent of GDP). Gross public external debt was estimated at 23 percent of GDP at the end of 2021, of\nwhich roughly one-quarter was comprised of holdings of local currency government bonds.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 1941,
      "end_char": 2076,
      "text": "Assessment. While the NIIP is sustainable and a relatively high share of the local currency denomination of foreign public liabilities\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2076,
      "end_char": 2215,
      "text": "reduces FX risks, the large gross foreign portfolio liabilities could be a source of vulnerability in case of global financial volatility.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2215,
      "end_char": 2370,
      "text": "Vulnerabilities from exchange rate volatility are moderate, as most Mexican firms with FX debt have natural hedges and actively\nmanage their FX exposures.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2370,
      "end_char": 2597,
      "text": "2021 (% GDP) NIIP: –41 Gross Assets: 58 Debt Assets: 19 Gross Liab.: 99 Debt Liab.: 38\nCurrent Background. In 2021, the CA balance moved to a deficit of 0.4 percent of GDP after posting a surplus of 2.5 percent of GDP in 2020,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 2597,
      "end_char": 2736,
      "text": "Account reflecting the recovery of import demand, including from the restocking of intermediate goods, with economic recovery. An increase\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 2736,
      "end_char": 2862,
      "text": "in investment and a decline in saving contributed roughly equally to the change in the CA balance in 2021. The private sector\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00019",
      "start_char": 2862,
      "end_char": 2988,
      "text": "saving-investment balance declined by 3.1 percentage points of GDP, more than offsetting the improvement in the public sector\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00020",
      "start_char": 2988,
      "end_char": 3116,
      "text": "balance of 0.7 percentage point. In 2022, the current account deficit is expected to widen with higher global commodity prices,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00021",
      "start_char": 3116,
      "end_char": 3245,
      "text": "given Mexico’s net commodity importer status. Other direct trade effects of the war in Ukraine are expected to be insignificant,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00022",
      "start_char": 3245,
      "end_char": 3378,
      "text": "given the limited trade linkages with Russia and other countries in eastern Europe. In addition, the domestic fuel price ceiling and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00023",
      "start_char": 3378,
      "end_char": 3512,
      "text": "the associated fuel subsidies will weaken the substitution and income effects of higher oil prices and amplify their impact on the CA\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00024",
      "start_char": 3512,
      "end_char": 3640,
      "text": "balance. Taking these factors into consideration, the 2022 CA deficit is projected to increase to around 1 percent of GDP, with\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00025",
      "start_char": 3640,
      "end_char": 3846,
      "text": "considerable forecast uncertainty, given risks from the COVID-19 pandemic and the war in Ukraine. Over the medium term, the CA\nbalance is projected to stabilize around a deficit of around 1 percent of GDP.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00026",
      "start_char": 3846,
      "end_char": 3974,
      "text": "Assessment. The EBA model estimates a cyclically adjusted CA norm of –1.2 percent of GDP in 2021. This implies a CA gap of –0.2\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00027",
      "start_char": 3974,
      "end_char": 4101,
      "text": "percent of GDP, with a range from –1.2 to 0.8 percent of GDP. The contribution from the overall policy gap is estimated at 1.3\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00028",
      "start_char": 4101,
      "end_char": 4237,
      "text": "percent of GDP, driven by the fiscal gap (1.2 percent). The latter reflects the relatively more accommodative fiscal stances of trading\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00029",
      "start_char": 4237,
      "end_char": 4365,
      "text": "partners. IMF staff adjustments were made to account for the transitory impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on travel services (0.1\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00030",
      "start_char": 4365,
      "end_char": 4493,
      "text": "percent of GDP), the transport balance (0.6 percent of GDP), the household consumption composition shift (–0.3 percent of GDP),\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00031",
      "start_char": 4493,
      "end_char": 4720,
      "text": "trade in medical products (0.1 percent of GDP), and remittances (–0.3 percent of GDP). Including these adjustments, the IMF staff\nassesses the midpoint CA gap at –0.2 percent of GDP, with a range of –1.2 to 0.8 percent of GDP.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00032",
      "start_char": 4720,
      "end_char": 4955,
      "text": "2021 (% GDP) CA: –0.4 Cycl. Adj. CA: –1.5 EBA Norm: –1.2 EBA Gap: -0.2 COVID-19 Adj.: 0.1 Other Adj.: 0 Staff Gap: -0.2\nReal Exchange Background. In 2021, the peso fluctuated in a narrow range of around 20 to 21 pesos per U.S. dollar. "
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00033",
      "start_char": 4955,
      "end_char": 5172,
      "text": "The average REER in 2021\nRate appreciated by about 6 percent compared to the 2020 average, mostly driven by a nominal appreciation. As of July 2022, the REER\nhad appreciated by 3 percent compared to its 2021 average.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00034",
      "start_char": 5172,
      "end_char": 5300,
      "text": "Assessment. The IMF staff CA gap implies a REER gap of 0.5 percent (applying a semi-elasticity of 0.33). The EBA REER level and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00035",
      "start_char": 5300,
      "end_char": 5435,
      "text": "index models estimate an overvaluation of 7.7 percent and an undervaluation of 9.1 percent, respectively, in 2021. IMF staff’s overall\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00036",
      "start_char": 5435,
      "end_char": 5649,
      "text": "assessment, based on the CA gap, is a REER gap in the range of –2.6 to 3.5 percent, with a midpoint of 0.5 percent.\nCapital and Background. In 2021, Mexico recorded a small amount of net financial account inflows. "
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00037",
      "start_char": 5649,
      "end_char": 5827,
      "text": "Net portfolio outflows increased compared\nFinancial to the previous year on account of both higher purchases of foreign assets by residents and larger sales of Mexican assets by\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00038",
      "start_char": 5827,
      "end_char": 5962,
      "text": "Accounts: nonresidents. The outflows were offset by a turnaround in other investment flows and continued strong net FDI inflows.\nFlows\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00039",
      "start_char": 5962,
      "end_char": 6097,
      "text": "Assessment. The long maturity of sovereign debt and the relatively high share of local-currency-denominated debt reduce the\nand Policy\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00040",
      "start_char": 6097,
      "end_char": 6229,
      "text": "exposure of government finances to FX depreciation and refinancing risks. The banking sector is resilient, and FX risks of\nMeasures\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00041",
      "start_char": 6229,
      "end_char": 6438,
      "text": "nonfinancial corporate debt are generally covered by natural and financial hedges. However, the strong presence of foreign\ninvestors leaves Mexico exposed to capital flow reversals and risk premium increases.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00042",
      "start_char": 6438,
      "end_char": 6576,
      "text": "FX Intervention Background. The central bank remains committed to a free-floating exchange rate and uses discretionary FX intervention to\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00043",
      "start_char": 6576,
      "end_char": 6721,
      "text": "and Reserves prevent disorderly market conditions. At the end of 2021, gross international reserves were USD 208 billion (16 percent of GDP), up\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00044",
      "start_char": 6721,
      "end_char": 6862,
      "text": "Level from USD 199 billion at the end of 2020, largely owing to the IMF’s general SDR allocation. In 2021, no FX intervention was\nconducted.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00045",
      "start_char": 6862,
      "end_char": 6988,
      "text": "Assessment. At 131 percent of the ARA metric and 254 percent of short-term debt (at remaining maturity), the level of foreign\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00046",
      "start_char": 6988,
      "end_char": 7228,
      "text": "reserves at the end of 2021 remains adequate. IMF staff recommend that the authorities continue to maintain reserves at an\nadequate level over the medium term. The Flexible Credit Line arrangement continues to provide an additional buffer.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00047",
      "start_char": 7228,
      "end_char": 7258,
      "text": "INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 53"
    }
  ]
}

{
